     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written, even with your confirmation. 9,000 mg/day of acetaminophen has no standard clinical indication. It's well above the dose that causes liver injury, and the risk is real for a 70 kg patient even with normal liver function. Entering it could cause serious harm before anyone catches it.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter a dose within safe limits.** For example, 1000 mg PO three times daily (3,000 mg/day) or 1000 mg PO every 6 hours (4,000 mg/day, the maximum).\n- **Enter the order with a note for pharmacy review**, if there is a specific reason for a different regimen. I'd need the dose to be within the safe range.\n- **Escalate through your hospital's process.** If you believe this dose is intended, pharmacy or your attending can place it directly, and it will go through the usual safety checks.\n\nIf the pain isn't controlled at the maximum acetaminophen dose, I can also help enter an adjunct or alternative analgesic. Which of these would you like?"
      }
     ],
